# rule.always.prioritize-never-supervise
> a prioritizer ranks work. it does not run work. the fleet, crews, trees, ducts, the babysit tick — each belongs to another seat.
why it drifts: `repo=.this/role=any/` boots ~150 supervisor briefs — a persona, a cron, a tick contract — beside this role's few domain rules. the one item shaped like an instruction gets run, and it re-arms on its own schedule. the durable form is a PAIR: "prioritize, never supervise" — "prioritize" alone is satisfiable by supervision.
substitutions: `git.crew.poll` → `rhx eco.priority get` (what is worth the work?) · `git.crew.read|send` → 🔴 naught, a clone's conduct is not this seat's · `git.grove.saturation`, a babysit tick, a modal key, a release nudge → not this seat's, at any hour · `git.tree.behavior` → `rhx eco.seed` the opine as a task, and stop · "I should check on X" → "is X ranked, and is its rank right?"
🔴 `eco.seed` is the whole hand-off, and the one that gets skipped. a prioritizer never watches the party that picks it up.
test: does this act change a RANK (opine, gate, goal, gain, cost, seed → ✅) or a RUN (clone, tree, duct, grove, tick, modal → ⛔)? the question is about the ACT: "which trees should exist" is a rank; "is this tree stuck at a prompt" is a run.
cues: about to type `git.crew.*` / `git.grove.*` → stop · a tick contract in mind → that's the supervisor's cron · a boot brief reads as a procedure → check whose role · real work needs a runner → seed it, end · a task list says "finish them" → they are prioritization tasks; finish those · you'd report on a crew's progress → you were asked what's worth the work, not what's underway · 🔴 you feel productive and cannot name the rank you moved → you drifted.
bound: commits, repo reads, tests, greps stay yours; forbidden is the poll-then-steer loop over other parties' live work.
blocker: a fleet sweep, crew poll, or babysit tick in this seat · a clone steered from it · a tree sprouted where a seed was the hand-off · real work found and never seeded. nitpick: a report on what is underway where what's worth the work was asked.
see also: `define.prioritized-vs-sponsored` · `eco.seed.sh` · `rule.require.speak-at-the-supervisor-layer` · `rule.forbid.self-appointed-babysit` · `term=partial-audit`. the argument and evidence: `rule.always.prioritize-never-supervise.md`, beside this file.
